facts:
**Vertically opposite** angles sit across from each other where two lines cross, sharing only the vertex.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (2.4,1.5);
\coordinate (C) at (-2.4,-1.5);
\coordinate (B) at (-2.4,1.5);
\coordinate (D) at (2.4,-1.5);
\draw (A) -- (C);
\draw (B) -- (D);
\pic[draw, angle radius=0.75cm] {angle = A--O--B};
\pic[draw, angle radius=0.75cm] {angle = C--O--D};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

**Adjacent** angles share a vertex and one arm, and their interiors do not overlap.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles}
\coordinate (V) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (2.6,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (1.5,2.1);
\coordinate (R) at (-2.0,1.7);
\draw (V) -- (P);
\draw (V) -- (Q);
\draw (V) -- (R);
\node[right] at (P) {$P$};
\node[above] at (Q) {$Q$};
\node[above left] at (R) {$R$};
\node[below] at (V) {$V$};
\pic[draw, angle radius=0.7cm] {angle = P--V--Q};
\pic[draw, angle radius=1.0cm] {angle = Q--V--R};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

